The attached image displays hematoxylin and eosin-stained sections of skeletal muscle from injection sites collected two weeks after immunization, comparing the local inflammatory response induced by aluminum hydroxide adjuvant (AH, left) and Nano-11 (right). The left panel shows extensive granulomatous inflammation (asterisk) with dense aggregates of macrophages and scattered eosinophils. In contrast, the right panel, representing Nano-11, demonstrates significantly less residual inflammation (asterisk) with only a few macrophages arranged in slender cords within the connective tissue. This indicates that Nano-11 induces a more transient and less persistent inflammatory response at the injection site compared to aluminum hydroxide, supporting its favorable safety profile as a vaccine adjuvant.